problems in preterm neonates who have no major congenital anomalies. STUDY DESIGN Retrospective review of the Pediatrix 
neonatal intensive care patient clinical data warehouse. RESULT The study cohort consisted of neonates born with an 
estimated gestational age of ≤ 30 completed weeks in whom there was no report of any major anomalies (n=66,526). In this
group of 66,526 neonates, there were 64,030 (96.2%) with no report of renal dysfunction or failure, 1239 (1.9%) in whom 
there was a diagnosis of renal dysfunction and 1257 infants (1.9%) with a diagnosis of renal failure. The clinical 
circumstances most strongly associated with a diagnosis of renal dysfunction and/or renal failures were low gestational 
age and birth weight. In addition, multivariate analysis showed that the factors associated with an increased risk of 
renal problems were vasopressor use during the first 7 days after birth, grade 3 or 4 intraventricular hemorrhage, a 
