│ labour and termination of pregnancy [3,4]. Considering the inventiveness of obstetricians in searching for new       │
│ agents to control preterm labour (Table 17.1), one would have expected that inhibitors of prostaglandin synthesis —  │
│ since they were freely available — would have been applied immediately to the problem of preterm labour. This did    │
│ not happen, however [5,6]. The first English language report on the use of a prostaglandin synthesis inhibitor for   │
│ treatment in preterm labour appeared only in 1974 [7]. This was after a retrospective study had shown prolongation   │
│ of pregnancy in chronic aspirin users [8] and after it was demonstrated that inhibitors of prostaglandin synthesis   │
│ prolonged the induction-abortion interval in women undergoing second trimester abortion by hypertonic saline [9]. In │
